WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.570
Humans have been altering plants and
animals for thousands of years;

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.830
we started with a process called "selective
breeding" where organisms that expressed

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:18.840
desired traits are bred together to pass
on those traits to their offspring.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.970
The result of this breeding is all around
you! For example, early farmers bred for

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:28.380
larger and tastier corn by selectively
planting only corn kernels that came

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.600
from corn that was larger and tastier. As
humans learned more about the role of

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.159
genes and DNA and trait expression, we
started developing new techniques for

00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:45.089
alterations. GMOs or genetically modified
organisms, are created by introducing a

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:49.620
gene from another organism or a gene
that has been modified in a lab.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.140
For example biotechnologists have created
varieties of corn that are resistant to

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.199
the herbicide Round-up by introducing a
modified version of a corn gene.

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:06.540
Recently, scientists have developed a whole new
way to alter DNA without introducing new

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.539
or modified genes; that tool is called
CRISPR-Cas9. Now, scientists can

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:16.859
alter corn to be resistant to Round-up by
simply changing the existing genetic

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:21.600
makeup of corn. This is much more
efficient and precise than the method of

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.600
introducing transgenes. So what is CRISPR ca9?

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.369
Cas9 is a protein that comes from bacteria like
streptococcus pyogenes and functions as

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.710
a defense against viruses. A virus
invades of bacterium by inserting its

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:43.740
genetic material into the bacterium's DNA
to make more copies of itself. When

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.270
infected with a virus, the bacterium
stores a small piece of viral DNA so

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.189
that the next time the virus attacks it
can produce an RNA copy which will guide

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:58.950
Cas9 to the invading viral DNA
Cas9 then makes a single

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:05.310
double-stranded cut in the viral genome. Learning from streptococcus, scientists

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.360
have been able to take advantage of this
precise defense mechanism to create

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.810
specific edits to
genome. Let's see how this would work if

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.390
scientists wanted to change a mouse's
fur color from black to white. Scientists

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:23.050
can provide Cas9 with an RNA
complement containing the gene they want

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:29.410
to modify, so that Cas9 knows what
DNA to look for. Here, our scientist

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:33.400
provides Cas 9 with the RNA complement
of the black fur color gene and

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.890
inserts it into a mouse embryo. Cas 9
that makes a single cuts in the gene, and

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:44.920
scientists can make a desired mutation
in one of two ways. The first way is just

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:50.560
to hope that the edit you want occurs by
chance; this works surprisingly well. The

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.080
cut ends of the DNA are fairly unstable,
and accidental insertions deletions, and

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.390
replacements are common. Scientists
perform CRISPR in a large number of

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.810
organisms, and then screen the
organisms for the desired

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:10.330
characteristics. The second way is to
provide the organism with a template of

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:14.980
the gene with the desired mutation, so that
repair enzymes in the organism can use

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.900
the template to make exactly the
mutation provided. The embryo is placed

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:26.260
back into a mother to finish development,
and is born with white fur. Now that we

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.400
understand CRISPR-Cas9, we can
envision a myriad of potential

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.110
applications. Gene therapy could be done
in embryos, so that babies grow up

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:40.150
without ever experiencing a disease. Gene
therapy could also potentially be done

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.320
in adults through edits to stem cells,
which are cells that can develop into

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.330
any of the specialized cells in the body
under the right conditions. Genetic

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:55.210
diseases such as sickle-cell and cystic
fibrosis could be prevented. Researchers

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.260
are also experimenting with a new
application of CRISPR to achieve gene

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.210
drive, a mechanism that ensures both
chromosomes in an organism received the

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.920
same edit so that all of their offspring,
and all their offspring's offspring

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.600
inherit the mutation, with the goal of
forcing that mutation to all members of

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:20.020
a population. For example, researchers
could eliminate malaria using gene Drive

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:25.260
in mosquitoes
CRISPR is the most powerful gene- editing

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.960
tool ever seen to date, but it is still
in a highly experimental phase. There are

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.370
no fully developed laws regulating
CRISPR, and there are many concerns about

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.590
both its practical and moral
consequences. As technology continues to

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:45.540
advance, researchers, lawmakers and the
public will have to decide how to

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.010
properly use CRISPR.

